text:
HTMX swaps a piece of the page with HTML the server returns. Write that piece as a partial, and you can render it in two places: inside the full page on the first load, and on its own when HTMX asks for it.
The fragment, todos/list.pt:
&lt;ul id="todos"&gt;
  &lt;li tal:repeat="todo todos"&gt;${todo}&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
The page, todos/index.pt, includes it:
&lt;h1&gt;Todos&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;form hx-post="/todos" hx-target="#todos" hx-swap="outerHTML"&gt;
  &lt;input name="title"&gt;&lt;button&gt;Add&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;
${render_partial('todos/list.pt', todos=todos)}
The views render the page on GET and only the fragment on POST:

import flask

import chameleon_rs
import chameleon_rs.flask as chameleon_flask

app = flask.Flask(__name__)
chameleon_flask.configure(template_folder=template_folder)
todos = ['Write docs']


@app.get('/todos')
@chameleon_flask.template('todos/index.pt')
def index():
    return {'todos': todos}


@app.post('/todos')
def add():
    todos.append(flask.request.form['title'])
    return chameleon_rs.render_partial('todos/list.pt', todos=todos).html_text


client = app.test_client()
print(client.post('/todos', data={'title': 'Ship it'}).text)

&lt;ul id="todos"&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;Write docs&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;Ship it&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;


chameleon_rs.render_partial() returns an HTML object. Its html_text is the markup. Because the fragment is one template file, the page and the HTMX response can’t drift apart.
chameleon_flask.response('todos/list.pt', todos=todos) does the same and returns a flask.Response, if you’d rather set a content type or status code.

An email needs an HTML body and a plain-text one. Render both from templates, with PageTemplate for HTML and PageTextTemplate for text. A text template uses the same ${...} expressions but doesn’t escape HTML or parse tags.

from chameleon_rs import PageTemplate, PageTextTemplate

html_body = PageTemplate("""\
&lt;p&gt;Hi ${user},&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Your order &lt;strong&gt;#${order.id}&lt;/strong&gt; has shipped:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li tal:repeat="item order.items"&gt;${item}&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;
""")

text_body = PageTextTemplate("""\
Hi ${user},

Your order #${order.id} has shipped:
${'\\n'.join('- ' + item for item in order.items)}
""")


class Order:
    id = 1042
    items = ['Rust & Python mug', 'Sticker &lt;3']


print(html_body(user='Ada', order=Order()))
print(text_body(user='Ada', order=Order()))

&lt;p&gt;Hi Ada,&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Your order &lt;strong&gt;#1042&lt;/strong&gt; has shipped:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;Rust &amp; Python mug&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Sticker &lt;3&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;

Hi Ada,

Your order #1042 has shipped:
- Rust & Python mug
- Sticker &lt;3



The HTML body escapes & and &lt;, and the text body leaves them alone.
In an app, keep the email templates in your template folder (for example emails/), and load them with PageTemplateLoader, or PageTextTemplateFile for the text version. Emails can use partials too. render_partial works in any template, so the footer you share with the site can live in one file:
&lt;p&gt;Hi ${user},&lt;/p&gt;
...
${render_partial('emails/_footer.pt', unsubscribe_url=unsubscribe_url)}
Then pass the two strings to your mail library, such as email.message.EmailMessage’s set_content() and add_alternative(..., subtype='html').

Examples
Test a variable before inserting it:
&lt;p tal:condition="request.message" tal:content="request.message" /&gt;
Testing for odd/even in a repeat-loop:
&lt;div tal:repeat="item range(10)"&gt;
  &lt;p tal:condition="repeat.item.even"&gt;Even&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p tal:condition="repeat.item.odd"&gt;Odd&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

Examples
Inserting the user name:
&lt;p tal:content="user.getUserName()"&gt;Fred Farkas&lt;/p&gt;
Inserting HTML/XML:
&lt;p tal:content="structure context.getStory()"&gt;
   Marked &lt;b&gt;up&lt;/b&gt; content goes here.
&lt;/p&gt;

Examples
&lt;ul tal:switch="item.type"&gt;
  &lt;li tal:case="'document'"&gt;
    Document
  &lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li tal:case="'folder'"&gt;
    Folder
  &lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li tal:case="default"&gt;
    Other
  &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

Examples
Unconditionally omitting a tag:
&lt;div tal:omit-tag="" comment="This tag will be removed"&gt;
  &lt;i&gt;...but this text will remain.&lt;/i&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
Conditionally omitting a tag:
&lt;b tal:omit-tag="not:bold"&gt;I may be bold.&lt;/b&gt;
The above example will omit the b tag if the variable bold is false.
Creating ten paragraph tags, with no enclosing tag:
&lt;span tal:repeat="n range(10)"
      tal:omit-tag=""&gt;
  &lt;p tal:content="n"&gt;1&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;

Examples
Iterating over a sequence of strings:
&lt;p tal:repeat="txt ('one', 'two', 'three')"&gt;
   &lt;span tal:replace="txt" /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
Inserting a sequence of table rows, and using the repeat variable to number the rows:
&lt;table&gt;
  &lt;tr tal:repeat="item here.cart"&gt;
      &lt;td tal:content="repeat.item.number"&gt;1&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;td tal:content="item.description"&gt;Widget&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;td tal:content="item.price"&gt;$1.50&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
Nested repeats:
&lt;table border="1"&gt;
  &lt;tr tal:repeat="row range(10)"&gt;
    &lt;td tal:repeat="column range(10)"&gt;
      &lt;span tal:define="x repeat.row.number;
                        y repeat.column.number;
                        z x * y"
            tal:replace="string:$x * $y = $z"&gt;1 * 1 = 1&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
Grouping objects by type, drawing a rule between elements of different types:
&lt;div tal:repeat="(type,objects) list(map(lambda g: (g[0], list(g[1])), itertools.groupby(objects, key=lambda o: o.meta_type)))"
     tal:define="itertools import:itertools"&gt;
  &lt;h2 tal:content="type"&gt;Meta Type&lt;/h2&gt;
  &lt;p tal:repeat="object objects"
     tal:content="object.id"&gt;Object ID&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;hr /&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;






Caution



It is important to fully realize the iterator produced by itertools.groupby, as well as the iterator produced for each group, in the expression passed to tal:repeat. This is because the implementation of certain repeat variables, such as length and end requires Chameleon to look ahead in the iterator, consuming it faster than is visible. The iterator returned by itertools.groupby is shared among all of its subgroups, so without the full reification of all the iterators, incorrect results will be produced.

Examples
Inserting a title:
&lt;span tal:replace="context.title"&gt;Title&lt;/span&gt;
Inserting HTML/XML:
&lt;div tal:replace="structure table" /&gt;

New expression types
The structure expression wraps an expression result as structure:
&lt;div&gt;${structure: body.text}&lt;/div&gt;
The import expression imports module globals:
&lt;div tal:define="compile import: re.compile"&gt;
  ...
&lt;/div&gt;
The load expression loads templates relative to the current template:
&lt;div tal:define="compile load: main.pt"&gt;
  ...
&lt;/div&gt;

Dictionary lookup as fallback after attribute error
If attribute lookup (using the obj.&lt;name&gt; syntax) raises an AttributeError exception, a secondary lookup is attempted using dictionary lookup: obj['&lt;name&gt;'].
Chameleon does this by rewriting all attribute lookups to a custom lookup call, and chameleon-rs does the same lookup natively:
def lookup_attr(obj, key):
    try:
        return getattr(obj, key)
    except AttributeError as exc:
        try:
            get = obj.__getitem__
        except AttributeError:
            raise exc
        try:
            return get(key)
        except KeyError:
            raise exc

Inline string substitution
In element attributes and in the text or tail of an element, string expression interpolation is available using the ${...} syntax:
&lt;span class="content-${item_type}"&gt;
   ${title or item_id}
&lt;/span&gt;

Code blocks
Using &lt;?python ... ?&gt; notation, you can embed Python statements in your templates:
&lt;div&gt;
  &lt;?python numbers = map(str, range(1, 10)) ?&gt;
  Please input a number from the range ${", ".join(numbers)}.
&lt;/div&gt;

Partials (chameleon-rs)
chameleon-rs adds a built-in render_partial function to every template, which renders another template file into the page:
&lt;div tal:repeat="item items"&gt;
  ${render_partial('shared/card.pt', item=item)}
&lt;/div&gt;
It’s the API of the chameleon_partials package, built in. See Partials.

Template arguments
Arguments passed by keyword to the render- or call method are inserted directly into the template execution namespace. This is different from ZPT where these are only available through the options dictionary.
Zope:
&lt;div tal:content="options/title" /&gt;
Chameleon:
&lt;div tal:content="title" /&gt;

Adapted from Chameleon’s language reference (source), modified for chameleon-rs. Used under Chameleon’s license; see LICENSE.txt and COPYRIGHT.txt in the repository.




${...} operator
The ${...} notation is short-hand for text insertion. The Python-expression inside the braces is evaluated and the result included in the output (all inserted text is escaped by default):
&lt;div id="section-${index + 1}"&gt;
  ${content}
&lt;/div&gt;
To escape this behavior, double the dollar sign: $${...} renders as the literal text ${...}.






Note



Older versions of Chameleon also accepted a backslash, \${...}. That is no longer supported (Chameleon 3.x changelog): the backslash is output as is and the expression is still evaluated.


Note that if an object implements the __html__ method, the result of this method will be inserted as-is (without XML escaping).


Code blocks
The &lt;?python ... ?&gt; notation allows you to embed Python code in templates:
&lt;div&gt;
  &lt;?python numbers = map(str, range(1, 10)) ?&gt;
  Please input a number from the range ${", ".join(numbers)}.
&lt;/div&gt;
The scope of name assignments is up to the nearest macro definition, or the template, if macros are not used.
Note that code blocks can span multiple lines and start on the next line of where the processing instruction begins:
&lt;?python
  foo = [1, 2, 3]
?&gt;
You can use this to debug templates:
&lt;div&gt;
  &lt;?python import pdb; pdb.set_trace() ?&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


Markup comments
You can apply the “!” and “?” modifiers to change how comments are processed:

Drop

&lt;!--! This comment will be dropped from output --&gt;

Verbatim

&lt;!--? This comment will be included verbatim --&gt;
That is, evaluation of ${...} expressions is disabled if the comment opens with the “?” character. The “?” itself is removed from the output.


In an ordinary comment, ${...} expressions are evaluated.

chameleon-rs implements Chameleon’s template language unchanged: the same TAL attributes, METAL macros, i18n attributes, ${...} interpolation and expression types, with the same output. Templates you wrote for Chameleon work as they are.
This page is a quick tour. The language reference covers every statement in detail.

A tour in one template

from chameleon_rs import PageTemplate

page = PageTemplate("""
&lt;section tal:define="count len(items)"&gt;
  &lt;h2&gt;${title} &lt;small tal:condition="count"&gt;(${count})&lt;/small&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
  &lt;p tal:condition="not count"&gt;Nothing here yet.&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li tal:repeat="item items"
        class="${'odd' if repeat.item.odd else 'even'}"
        tal:content="item.upper()"&gt;placeholder&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;/ul&gt;
  &lt;a href="${url}" tal:attributes="title url"&gt;More&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;
""")

print(page(title='Tags', items=['rust', 'python', 'html'], url='/tags'))


&lt;section&gt;
  &lt;h2&gt;Tags &lt;small&gt;(3)&lt;/small&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;

  &lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li
        class="even"&gt;RUST&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li
        class="odd"&gt;PYTHON&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li
        class="even"&gt;HTML&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;/ul&gt;
  &lt;a href="/tags" title="/tags"&gt;More&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;





The pieces







Syntax
What it does




${expr}
Insert a value, HTML-escaped. Works in text and in attribute values.


${structure: expr}
Insert markup without escaping. Objects with __html__ are also inserted as-is.


tal:define="name expr"
Bind a variable for the element and its children (global for the rest of the template).


tal:condition="expr"
Keep the element only if the expression is true.


tal:repeat="item items"
Repeat the element for each item. repeat.item gives index, number, odd, even, start, end, length, …


tal:content="expr" / tal:replace="expr"
Replace the element’s content, or the whole element.


tal:attributes="name expr; ..."
Set or remove attributes.


tal:omit-tag=""
Drop the element’s tags and keep its content.


tal:switch / tal:case
Choose one of several branches.


tal:on-error="expr"
Render a fallback if the element raises.


metal:define-macro / metal:use-macro / metal:fill-slot
Layouts and reusable fragments. See below.


i18n:translate, i18n:attributes, i18n:name, i18n:domain
Mark text for translation.



Expressions are Python by default. Other expression types are written with a prefix: string:, exists:, not:, import:, load: and structure:.


Layouts with METAL
A layout defines a macro with slots, and each page fills them:

layout = PageTemplate("""
&lt;html metal:define-macro="layout"&gt;
  &lt;head&gt;&lt;title metal:define-slot="title"&gt;Site&lt;/title&gt;&lt;/head&gt;
  &lt;body&gt;&lt;main metal:define-slot="content"&gt;&lt;/main&gt;&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
""")

page = PageTemplate("""
&lt;html metal:use-macro="layout.macros['layout']"&gt;
  &lt;title metal:fill-slot="title"&gt;Home&lt;/title&gt;
  &lt;main metal:fill-slot="content"&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome, ${user}.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/main&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
""")

print(page(layout=layout, user='Ada'))


&lt;html&gt;
  &lt;head&gt;&lt;title&gt;Home&lt;/title&gt;&lt;/head&gt;
  &lt;body&gt;&lt;main&gt;&lt;p&gt;Welcome, Ada.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/main&gt;&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;



In a template loaded from a file, load: loads the layout relative to the page: metal:use-macro="load: ../shared/_layout.pt".


Partials
chameleon-rs adds one builtin, render_partial, to every template: ${render_partial('shared/card.pt', item=item)}. See Partials.

This page installs chameleon-rs, renders a template from a string, then sets up a folder of template files the way a web app would.

Install
uv pip install chameleon-rs
chameleon-rs supports CPython 3.12 and later, including the free-threaded build. The wheel contains the compiled engine and has no dependencies.


Render a template string
PageTemplate compiles a template from a string. Calling it renders the template, with the keyword arguments as the template’s variables:

from chameleon_rs import PageTemplate

page = PageTemplate("""
&lt;div class="greeting"&gt;
  &lt;h1&gt;Hello, ${name}!&lt;/h1&gt;
  &lt;ul tal:condition="items"&gt;
    &lt;li tal:repeat="item items"&gt;${repeat.item.number}. ${item}&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
""")

print(page(name='World', items=['templates', 'in', 'Rust']))


&lt;div class="greeting"&gt;
  &lt;h1&gt;Hello, World!&lt;/h1&gt;
  &lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;1. templates&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;2. in&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;3. Rust&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;



The template compiles once, when it’s first rendered. Every later render reuses the compiled program, so keep template objects around (module level, or in a loader) rather than building one per request.
${...} inserts a value with HTML escaping. To insert markup as-is, use structure:, or pass an object with an __html__ method:

page = PageTemplate('&lt;p&gt;${text}&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;${structure: text}&lt;/p&gt;')
print(page(text='&lt;b&gt;bold&lt;/b&gt;'))

&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;bold&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;bold&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




Render templates from files
PageTemplateFile loads one file. PageTemplateLoader loads templates by name from one or more folders, and caches each compiled template:
Suppose your templates live in a folder like this:
templates/
├── home/
│   └── index.pt
└── shared/
    └── video.pt
with home/index.pt:
&lt;html&gt;
  &lt;body&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;${title}&lt;/h1&gt;
    &lt;div tal:repeat="video videos"&gt;
      ${render_partial('shared/video.pt', video=video)}
    &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
and shared/video.pt:
&lt;a href="/videos/${video.id}"&gt;${video.title}&lt;/a&gt;
Configure the folder once at startup with chameleon_rs.configure(), then load templates by name:

from dataclasses import dataclass

import chameleon_rs
from chameleon_rs import PageTemplateLoader


@dataclass
class Video:
    id: int
    title: str


chameleon_rs.configure(template_folder=template_folder)
templates = PageTemplateLoader([template_folder])

page = templates['home/index.pt']
print(page(title='Latest videos', videos=[Video(1, 'Intro'), Video(2, 'Partials')]))

&lt;html&gt;
  &lt;body&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;Latest videos&lt;/h1&gt;
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;a href="/videos/1"&gt;Intro&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;a href="/videos/2"&gt;Partials&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;



render_partial is available in every template, with nothing to register or pass in. configure() tells it where to find partials. See Partials.
In a real app, build the folder path relative to your code, so it doesn’t depend on the working directory:
from pathlib import Path

import chameleon_rs

template_folder = (Path(__file__).parent / 'templates').as_posix()
chameleon_rs.configure(template_folder=template_folder, auto_reload=dev_mode)
auto_reload=True recompiles a template when its file changes. Turn it on in development and leave it off in production.


Next steps

Already have a Chameleon app? See Switching from Chameleon.
Using Flask or Quart? See Flask and Quart.
The language reference covers TAL, METAL, i18n and expressions.

Examples
Simple macro definition:
&lt;p metal:define-macro="copyright"&gt;
  Copyright 2011, &lt;em&gt;Foobar&lt;/em&gt; Inc.
&lt;/p&gt;

Examples
Simple macro with slot:
&lt;p metal:define-macro="hello"&gt;
  Hello &lt;b metal:define-slot="name"&gt;World&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
This example defines a macro with one slot named name. When you use this macro you can customize the b element by filling the name slot.

Examples
Given this macro:
&lt;p metal:define-macro="hello"&gt;
  Hello &lt;b metal:define-slot="name"&gt;World&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
You can fill the name slot like so:
&lt;p metal:use-macro="container['master.html'].macros.hello"&gt;
  Hello &lt;b metal:fill-slot="name"&gt;Kevin Bacon&lt;/b&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

Examples
&lt;div metal:define-macro="page-header"
     metal:extend-macro="standard_macros['page-header']"&gt;
  &lt;div metal:fill-slot="breadcrumbs"&gt;
    You are here:
    &lt;div metal:define-slot="breadcrumbs"/&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

Examples
Basic string formatting:
&lt;span tal:replace="string:$this and $that"&gt;
  Spam and Eggs
&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;p tal:content="string:${request.form['total']}"&gt;
  total: 12
&lt;/p&gt;
Including a dollar sign:
&lt;p tal:content="string:$$$cost"&gt;
  cost: $42.00
&lt;/p&gt;

Examples
Loading a template and using it as a macro:
&lt;div tal:define="master load: ../master.pt" metal:use-macro="master" /&gt;

Most sites have one layout: the &lt;head&gt;, the navigation and the footer. Write it once as a METAL macro with slots, and have each page fill them.
The layout, shared/_layout.pt (the underscore marks it as not a page of its own):
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html lang="en" metal:define-macro="layout"&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;title&gt;&lt;tal:title metal:define-slot="title"&gt;My site&lt;/tal:title&gt;&lt;/title&gt;
  &lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="/static/site.css"&gt;
  &lt;tal:head metal:define-slot="head"&gt;&lt;/tal:head&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;nav&gt;&lt;a href="/"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt; &lt;span tal:condition="user"&gt;Hi, ${user}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/nav&gt;
  &lt;main metal:define-slot="content"&gt;&lt;/main&gt;
  &lt;tal:scripts metal:define-slot="scripts"&gt;&lt;/tal:scripts&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
A page, home/index.pt, loads the layout relative to itself with load: and fills the slots it needs:
&lt;html metal:use-macro="load: ../shared/_layout.pt"&gt;
  &lt;tal:title metal:fill-slot="title"&gt;Home | My site&lt;/tal:title&gt;
  &lt;main metal:fill-slot="content"&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;Welcome&lt;/h1&gt;
  &lt;/main&gt;
  &lt;script metal:fill-slot="scripts" src="/static/home.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;

from chameleon_rs import PageTemplateLoader

templates = PageTemplateLoader([template_folder])
print(templates['home/index.pt'](user='Ada'))

&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html lang="en"&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;title&gt;Home | My site&lt;/title&gt;
  &lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="/static/site.css"&gt;

&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;nav&gt;&lt;a href="/"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt; &lt;span&gt;Hi, Ada&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/nav&gt;
  &lt;main&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;Welcome&lt;/h1&gt;
  &lt;/main&gt;
  &lt;script src="/static/home.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;




A few things to note:

A slot the page doesn’t fill keeps the layout’s default content: head stays empty here.
The layout sees the page’s variables (user here), so pass layout data such as the signed-in user in every view’s model, or add it in one place in your framework.
tal: and metal: elements (&lt;tal:title&gt;) render only their content. They’re handy for slots that shouldn’t add a tag.
The layout is compiled once and cached by the loader, like any template.
